bash
while true; do
  echo -n "$(date +%T) "
  curl -s -u "$SQ_TOKEN:" "$SQ_URL/api/ce/activity_status" \
    | jq -c '{pending, inProgress, failing, pendingMin: (.pendingTime/60000 | floor)}'
  sleep 60
done

If pending ticks down, it's a queue depth problem — raise workers. If it's flat at 51 and pendingMin keeps climbing, the worker is wedged.

What's actually in the queue:

bash
# Breakdown by task type
curl -s -u "$SQ_TOKEN:" "$SQ_URL/api/ce/activity?status=PENDING&ps=100" \
  | jq -r '.tasks[].type' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn

# What's currently running, and since when
curl -s -u "$SQ_TOKEN:" "$SQ_URL/api/ce/activity?status=IN_PROGRESS" \
  | jq '.tasks[] | {type, componentName, startedAt, executionTimeMs}'

The failing task's stack trace:

bash
TASK_ID=$(curl -s -u "$SQ_TOKEN:" "$SQ_URL/api/ce/activity?status=FAILED&ps=1" | jq -r '.tasks[0].id')
curl -s -u "$SQ_TOKEN:" "$SQ_URL/api/ce/task?id=$TASK_ID&additionalFields=stacktrace" \
  | jq -r '.task.errorMessage, .task.errorStacktrace'

Two more worth having:

bash
# ES / DB health causes — catches the disk watermark case
curl -s -u "$SQ_TOKEN:" "$SQ_URL/api/system/health" | jq

# Full system info: heap, DB pool, CE worker count, all in one blob
curl -s -u "$SQ_TOKEN:" "$SQ_URL/api/system/info" > sysinfo-$(date +%s).json
jq '.["Compute Engine Tasks"], .["Compute Engine JVM State"]."Max Memory (MB)"' sysinfo-*.json
